 "action_summary": "The owner asks why the system cannot be shown an advanced coding action once and then do it. Until now every action the front door can do was written down by a writer; showing it an edit taught it nothing. This change adds one taught action that works an edit out from an example: two files, before and after. It finds the smallest pieces of code that changed, makes a part of a piece a blank only where two changed places differ, rejects any rule that would change code the example left alone, and repeats the edit in a third file, touching only the bytes of the places that fit. A change that only works together with other changes, such as a rename, is refused. The action is taught through the existing demonstration-to-skill compiler with a second example and runs inside the sandbox. Separately, the wrong PASS left open by the previous change is closed: a sentence that states a default for an added parameter now gets that default, or is refused. No registry row is added. No capability credit is claimed.",
